    93	## 3. Theorem TOPTAIL: the t quadratics on b4=1
    94	
    95	Write `r=2t-1-k` for the top tail `k=t..2t-1`, so `r=0` is the topmost row.
    96	
    97	**(a) b3-degree.**  `wt b3=t+1` and `wt T_(t,2t-1-r)=2t+2+r`; `3(t+1)>2t+2+r`
    98	for `r<=t-1`, so `deg_b3<=2`, and `deg_b3=2` exactly when `a_r != 0`.  The
    99	coefficient of `b3^2` is a weighted form of weight `r`, of `b3^1` weight
   100	`t+1+r`, of `b3^0` weight `2t+2+r` -- in `A_t[b4,q]` alone (a monomial with a
   101	`b3` factor is counted in the next coefficient).  Hence, with `b4=1`,
   102	
   103	```text
   104	Q_r(b3) = a_r b3^2 + b_r b3 + c_r,   a_r in F_r,  b_r in F_(t+1+r),  c_r in F_(2t+2+r),
   105	F_w := {polynomials in q_(2,0..t-1,0) of weight <= w}                (3.1)
   106	```
   107	
   108	(`b4=1` turns the grading into the filtration `F`; a monomial of weight `m<=w`
   109	is the image of `b4^(w-m)*monomial`).  Which `q`'s occur: `a_r` can only contain
   110	`q_(j,0)` with `j<=r`; `b_r` and `c_r` can contain every `q_(j,0)`, `j<=t-1`,
   111	since `t+1+r>=t-1`.  This is (2.14) of the charged Sol report specialised to
   112	the top tail, and it is exact, not a bound: at `t=3,4,5,6,7` **every** monomial
   113	of the allowed weight occurs in every one of the `3t` coefficients (the number
   114	of terms equals the number of weighted monomials -- e.g. `t=5`: `a`: 1,1,2,3,5;
   115	`b`: 9,11,15,18,23; `c`: 34,39,47,54,64 for `r=0..4`; `t=6`: `c`:
   116	70,84,101,119,141,164; `t=7`: `a`: 1,1,2,3,5,7,11, `b`: 20,26,35,44,58,71,90,
   117	`c`: 136,163,199,235,282,331,391).  So there is no sparsity to exploit in `b_r,c_r`.
   118	
   119	**(b) The leading coefficients are uniformly computable.**  The eliminated
   120	spine variables have the weights `wt C_j=j`, `wt q_(j,0)=j (t<=j<=2t)`,
   121	`wt b2=2t+1`, `wt b1=3t+1`, `wt T(0)=t`, and each solved value is a weighted
   122	form of its own weight in the residual variables (banked grading (2.1)).  A
   123	monomial of `[b3^2]T_(t,2t-1-r)` has `(b4,q)`-weight `r`.  A solved value can
   124	contribute to it only through its own monomials of `b3`-degree `<=2` and
   125	`(b4,q)`-weight `<=r`: for `C_j` (pure, weight `j<t+1`) that needs `j<=r`; for
   126	`q_(j,0)`, `t<=j<=2t`, the pure part has weight `j>=t>r`, and only its
   127	`b3`-linear part `b3*(weight j-t-1)` can enter, needing `j<=t+1+r`; `b2`'s
   128	`b3`-part has weight `t>r`; `T(0)` is pure of weight `t>r`; `b1`'s `b3^2`-part
   129	has weight `t-1`, entering only at `r=t-1`.  The same count applied to the band
   130	equations `rho_j+p_j z_j=0` shows that the `(b4,q)`-weight-`<=r` parts of
   131	`C_1..C_r` and the `b3`-linear parts of `q_(t+1,0)..q_(t+1+r,0)` are determined
   132	by each other alone.  Therefore, for `0<=r<=t-2`,
   133	
   134	```text
   135	a_r = [b3^2] T_(t,2t-1-r) is computed by the truncated spine of 2r+1 scalars
   136	      {C_1..C_r ; b3-parts of q_(t+1,0)..q_(t+1+r,0)},  all other spine
   137	      variables set to 0, and only the b4-degree-<=r window of the arrays
   138	      (binomials binom(q,m) with q-m<=r) enters.                        (3.2)
   139	```
   140	
   141	Every entry of that finite triangular solve is a polynomial in `t` of degree
   142	`<=r` over `A_t` divided by the banked unit pivots, so **for each fixed `r`,
   143	`a_r` is a single rational function of `(t,d)`**, valid for all `t>=r+2`.
   144	`r=0` is the b3-axis chart and gives `a_0=-alpha_t` (driver sign):
   145	
   146	```text
   147	alpha_t = t(3t+1)[(27t^3-30t^2+t-2)d+(6t^3+13t^2-3t+2)] / (12q^2(3t-1)^2(3t+2)),
   148	a unit of A_t for every t>=3 (banked norm (4.8)).                        (3.3)
   149	```
   150	
   151	checked here symbolically at `t=3,4,5,6,7`: the driver's `[b3^2]T_(t,2t-1)`
   152	plus `alpha_t` reduces to `0` in `A_t`.  `r=1` gives `a_1 = mu_t b4`, one
   153	scalar; `r=2` gives `a_2 = mu'_t b4^2 + mu''_t q_(2,0)`.  Their closed forms
   154	were **not** derived (that needs a symbolic-`t` engine for (3.2); typed OPEN),
   155	but the exact values are:
   156	
   157	| `t` | `Res_y(H_t, mu_t)` (`r=1`, coefficient of `b3^2 b4`) | `Res_y(H_t, mu'_t)` (`b3^2 b4^2`) | `Res_y(H_t, mu''_t)` (`b3^2 q_(2,0)`) |
   158	|---:|---|---|---|
   159	| 3 | `159028943/148574272` | `-4250137195970063/4803319854964400` | `-11399375/34603212` |
   160	| 4 | `-804680656/847247445` | `27103436928548351176/4441316531861763675` | `-1273869353120/1299812628609` |
   161	| 5 | `-109301941200/20300415751` | `56469816361018858800/29246083658911332521` | `-10644469200/3246539263` |
   162	| 6 | `-700061590663/53458672345` | `-85303805865939146725660/13070843663072030433637` | `-327293088530100/46095984051013` |
