   314	## 7. What is proved uniformly, and the sharpest partial statement
   315	
   316	For every `t>=3`: the top tail on `b4=1` is `t` quadratics `Q_r` with the
   317	structure (3.1)-(3.2); `a_0=alpha_t` is a unit; the finite projection reduces
   318	`(C0)` to `J cap A_t[q]=(1)`; Lemma SQUARE reduces `(V0)` to the `(t-1)`-form
   319	system `R_1..R_(t-1)` whose tail is inherited from the proved chain and whose
   320	head `(RH_t)` is `(C0)` for the resultants.  `(RH_t)` holds exactly at `t=3`
   321	and modulo a good prime at `t=4,5`.  `(C0)` itself holds at `t=2..7` (this
   322	lane exactly at `t=2,3,4`; `t=5,6,7` through the banked promotion of `(V0)`).
   323	
   324	Sharpest partial statement: **`(C0)` for all `t>=3` follows from a single
   325	unit test in `t-2` variables, `(RH_t)`, whose `t-1` generators are explicit
   326	isobaric polynomials (5.1) in the `3t` coefficients of Theorem TOPTAIL, and
   327	`(C0)` is equivalent to `J cap A_t[q]=(1)`; the `b4=0` tail of the resultant
   328	system is proved on the same sub-charts as the original chain, with the
   329	explicit units `alpha_t^2 (A2^(r))^2`, `r<=5`, `t>=3r+3`.**  What is missing is not another sub-chart but a
   330	certificate for an open condition; the cheapest fixed-`t` test remains the
   331	direct `b4=1` top-tail chart (`D3`, char 0), and the cheapest falsification
   332	test is `(RH_t)` modulo `p` (instant at `t<=5`).
