   131	The charged reports establish the exact b3-axis control:
   132	
   133	```text
   134	T_{t,2t-1}|axis = alpha_t b3^2,
   135	T_{t,t-2}|axis = beta_t b3^3,
   136	T_{t,0}|axis = -c,
   137	all other rows vanish on the axis,
   138	alpha_t,beta_t units for all t>=3 and both vanish exactly at t=2.
   139	```
   140	
   141	The exact `t=2` controls remain essential:
   142	
   143	```text
   144	t=2, y=1/5:
   145	  a_0=a_1=0, the cone has the b3-axis, (V0) fails,
   146	  but T0hom is in the radical with power 1, so (8.1) holds.
   147	
   148	t=2, y=2/5:
   149	  a_0=-28/625 is a unit, tail and full cone are zero-dimensional,
   150	  (V0) and (8.1) both hold.
   151	```
   152	
   153	## 3. Target A: Gamma And The Nonzerodivisor
   154	
   155	### 3.1 Exact Reformulation
   156	
   157	Let `N` be the `(t-1) x 2` matrix over `P_t` with rows `(C_r,B_r)`.  The charged
   158	FITT theorem identifies the 0-th Fitting ideal of `M_t/(G)M_t`, with
   159	`M_t=S_t/(T_{t,2t-1})`, as
   160	
   161	```text
   162	F_t = I_2(N) + (W_1,...,W_{t-1}) + (X_rs)_{r<s},
   163	W_r = a_0 C_r^2 - b_0 B_r C_r + c_0 B_r^2.
   164	```
   165	
   166	The explicit eliminant certificate, reproduced here in
   167	`box/k16nzd-20260903/eliminant_certificate.out`, is the identity
   168	
   169	```text
   170	W_r = B_r^2 T_{t,2t-1}
   171	      - G_r (a_0 G_r - 2 a_0 C_r + b_0 B_r).
   172	```
   173	
   174	The frozen driver prints
   175	
   176	```text
   177	W - [B^2 Q_0 - G(a0 G - 2a0 C + b0 B)] = 0
   178	Res_b3(Q_0,G) = B^2 c0 - B C b0 + C^2 a0 = W.
   179	```
   180	
   181	At a rank-1 point `p` of `N`, with kernel spanned by `(1:beta)`, we have
   182	`C_r(p)=-beta B_r(p)`.  Therefore
   183	
   184	```text
   185	W_r(p) = B_r(p)^2 Q_t(p,beta),
   186	X_rs(p) = B_r(p)B_s(p) Q_t(p,beta),
   187	Q_t(p,beta) = a_0 beta^2 + b_0 beta + c_0
   188	             = T_{t,2t-1}(p,beta).
   189	```
   190	
   191	Consequently, after clause (i) excludes nonzero rank-0 points, Target A is
   192	exactly:
   193	
   194	```text
   195	Q_t(p,beta) != 0
   196	for every nonzero p in Gamma = V(I_2(N)) with rank N(p)=1.
   197	```
   198	
   199	Equivalently, `T_{t,2t-1}` is a nonzerodivisor on
   200	`S_t/(G_1,...,G_{t-1})`.
   201	
   202	### 3.2 EN-Curve Parametrisation
   203	
   204	The usable parametrisation is the incidence description, not a closed formula
   205	for all coordinates.  Define
   206	
   207	```text
   208	Sigma_t = V(C_r + beta B_r, r=1..t-1)
   209	          inside Spec P_t x A^1_beta.
   210	```
   211	
   212	On the open where not all `B_r` vanish, projection
   213	`Sigma_t -> Gamma=V(I_2(N))` is an isomorphism over the rank-1 locus and beta is
   214	the kernel coordinate.  Clause (i), or the stronger charged B-HSOP statement,
   215	removes nonzero points with all `B_r=C_r=0`; it also removes the kernel
   216	`(0:1)` obstruction.  Thus the card's "kernel is `(1:beta)` and `(C_r,B_r)`
   217	are proportional" is exactly this incidence scheme.
   218	
   219	What is missing is a closed lower-dimensional base with closed-form coordinates
   220	for the rank-1 points.  The charged rank report explains why: the `B_r,C_r`
   221	have full support, and the `b_r,c_r` coefficients are spine-complete
   222	`t`-indexed sequences.  The EN complex gives the Hilbert series and weighted
   223	degree of the curve once the expected height is known, but it does not
   224	parametrise the individual points at which `Q_t` must be evaluated.
   225	
   226	The charged EN-CURVE formula is
   227	
   228	```text
   229	Hilb(P_t/I_2(N);s)
   230	 = [ prod_{r=1}^{t-1}(1-s^{2t+2+r})
   231	     - s^{t+1} prod_{r=1}^{t-1}(1-s^{t+1+r}) ]
   232	   / [ (1-s^{t+1}) prod_{i=1}^{t-1}(1-s^i) ],
   233	
   234	d_Gamma(t)
   235	 = [ C(3t+1,t-1) - C(2t,t-1) ] / (t+1).
   236	```
   237	
   238	This is a uniform structural theorem.  It reduces Target A to finitely many
   239	scalar nonvanishings per `t`, but it does not compute those scalars in closed
   240	form.
   241	
   242	### 3.3 Leading Coefficient Of The Quadratic
   243	
   244	The top row restricted to the incidence curve is
   245	
   246	```text
   247	Q_t(p,beta)=a_0 beta^2+b_0 beta+c_0,
   248	a_0=-alpha_t.
   249	```
   250	
   251	The charged alpha norm is
   252	
   253	```text
   254	Res_y(H_t, A_b d + B_b)
   255	 = -4 (t-2)(2t+1)^2(3t-1)^2(3t+2)
   256	     (27t^3+17t^2+t+2),
   257	```
   258	
   259	so `a_0` is a unit for every integer `t>=3` and on both factors at split
   260	indices.  This proves the quadratic is genuinely degree two in beta.  It does
   261	not prove it is nonzero at every Gamma point; the missing claim is not leading
   262	degree, but disjointness:
   263	
   264	```text
   265	Sigma_t cap V(Q_t) = {0}.
   266	```
   267	
   268	No all-t resultant or intersection certificate for this disjointness was found
   269	in the charged inputs or in this lane.
   270	
   271	### 3.4 LENGTH-SPLIT Does Not Force Target A
   272	
   273	The charged length identity is
   274	
   275	```text
   276	L_t = 2 C(3t+1,t-1)
   277	    = 2 C(2t,t-1) + (2t+2) d_Gamma(t).
   278	```
   279	
   280	The small CI driver was rerun here:
   281	`box/k16nzd-20260903/ci_series_identity_t16.out` prints
   282	`ALL_IDENTITY_CHECKS_PASS = True` through `t=16`, including
   283	
   284	```text
   285	L_3=90, L_4=572, L_5=3640, L_6=23256, L_7=149226,
   286	L_8=961400, L_9=6216210, L_10=40320150.
   287	```
   288	
   289	The identity has the following precise meaning.  If the relevant complete
   290	intersection is zero-dimensional, then its length decomposes into the b3-axis
   291	contribution
   292	
   293	```text
   294	2 C(2t,t-1)
   295	```
   296	
   297	and the residual Gamma contribution
   298	
   299	```text
   300	(2t+2) d_Gamma(t).
   301	```
   302	
   303	The factor `2` is the order of the top row on the b3-axis:
   304	`T_{t,2t-1}|axis = alpha_t b3^2`.  The factor `2t+2` is the weighted degree of
   305	the top row cutting each non-axis rank-1 line where the scalar `Q_t(p,beta)` is
   306	nonzero.
   307	
   308	This does not prove nonvanishing.  If `Q_t(p,beta)=0` on a Gamma component, then
   309	the top row vanishes identically on the corresponding residual line of
   310	`V(G_1,...,G_{t-1})`; the tail quotient is not a finite complete intersection
   311	there, and the finite length `L_t` is not available as a contradiction.  Using
   312	`L_t` to force the scalar nonvanishing would reverse the charged implication
   313	`dim=0 -> length=L_t`.  The charged DEGREE-BLIND theorem gives the exact
   314	control: at `t=2` the two fibres have identical weights and predicted length
   315	data, while `y=1/5` has dimension 1 and `y=2/5` has dimension 0.
   316	
   317	Thus LENGTH-SPLIT is a map of the residual problem, not a proof of Target A.
   318	
   319	### 3.5 Fixed-Range Computation
   320	
   321	The charged RANK report gives:
