   290	
   291	### 4.3 The splitting
   292	
   293	**Theorem TAIL-SPLIT.**  Let `t>=3`.  Then:
   294	
   295	1. `T_{t,2t-1} = a_0 b3^2 + b_0 b3 + c_0` with `a_0 in A_t^x`.  Hence
   296	   `T_{t,2t-1}` is a monic-up-to-unit quadratic in `b3` over `P_t`, is a
   297	   nonzerodivisor of `S_t`, and
   298	
   299	   ```text
   300	   M_t := S_t/(T_{t,2t-1}) is a FREE graded P_t-module with basis {1, b3},
   301	   Hilb(M_t; s) = (1+s^{t+1}) / prod_{i=1}^{t-1}(1-s^i),
   302	   dim M_t = t-1,  M_t Cohen-Macaulay.                                 (4.5)
   303	   ```
   304	
   305	2. Put `G_r := a_0 T_{t,2t-1-r} - a_r T_{t,2t-1}` for `r=1..t-1`.  Since `a_0` is
   306	   a unit, `J_t^tail = (T_{t,2t-1}, G_1, .., G_{t-1})`, and by construction the
   307	   `b3^2` terms cancel:
   308	
   309	   ```text
   310	   G_r = B_r b3 + C_r,  B_r = a_0 b_r - a_r b_0,  C_r = a_0 c_r - a_r c_0,
   311	   wt(B_r) = t+1+r,  wt(C_r) = 2t+2+r,  wt(G_r) = 2t+2+r.              (4.6)
   312	   ```
   313	
   314	3. Consequently
   315	
   316	   ```text
   317	   J_t^tail is an hsop of S_t
   318	     <=> (G_1,..,G_{t-1}) is an hsop of the (t-1)-dimensional CM ring M_t
   319	     <=> dim M_t/(G_1,..,G_{t-1}) = 0,
   320	   ```
   321	
   322	   and in that case, `M_t` being CM, the `G_r` are an `M_t`-regular sequence and
   323	
   324	   ```text
   325	   Hilb(S_t/J_t^tail; s) = (1+s^{t+1}) * prod_{r=1}^{t-1}(1-s^{2t+2+r})
   326	                                       / prod_{i=1}^{t-1}(1-s^i)
   327	                         = (1+s^{t+1}) [3t+1 choose t-1]_s = P_t(s).    (4.7)
   328	   ```
   329	
   330	*Proof.*  (1) is (4.1) plus (4.4).  In `S_t = P_t[b3]`, division with remainder
   331	by `T_{t,2t-1}` is possible and unique because its leading coefficient `a_0` is
   332	invertible; so every class in `M_t` has a unique representative of `b3`-degree
   333	`<= 1`, i.e. `{1,b3}` is a `P_t`-basis of `M_t`.  A free module is torsion-free,
   334	so `T_{t,2t-1}` is a nonzerodivisor; `M_t` is finite free over the regular ring
   335	`P_t`, hence Cohen-Macaulay of dimension `t-1`; and the graded basis degrees `0`
   336	and `t+1` give the stated series.  (2) is the definition; the
   337	`b3^2` coefficient of `G_r` is `a_0 a_r - a_r a_0 = 0`, and the substitution
   338	`T_{t,2t-1-r} = a_0^{-1}(G_r + a_r T_{t,2t-1})` shows the two generating sets
   339	agree.  (3): an ideal generated by `t-1` elements in a `(t-1)`-dimensional CM
   340	ring is an hsop iff the quotient is `0`-dimensional, and an hsop in a CM ring is
   341	a regular sequence; the Hilbert series of a quotient by a regular sequence is
   342	the stated product, and the last equality is Theorem CI-SERIES.  QED
   343	
   344	This is where the two factors of `P_t(s)` come from: `(1+s^{t+1})` is
   345	`rank_{P_t} M_t = deg_{b3} T_{t,2t-1} = 2` with the b3 shift, and
   346	`[3t+1 choose t-1]_s` is the CI series of `t-1` forms of the *consecutive*
   347	degrees `2t+3, .., 3t+1` in `t-1` variables of weights `1,..,t-1`.  So `L_t`'s
   348	factor `2` is not a fibre artefact: it is the generic degree of the finite flat
   349	projection `Spec M_t -> Spec P_t`, i.e. the b3-degree of the top tail row.
   350	
   351	**Verification.**  At `t=2,3,4,5`, on regenerated rows: every `G_r` has
   352	`deg_{b3} = 1` with `wt(B_r) = t+1+r` and `wt(C_r) = 2t+2+r` exactly as
   353	predicted (at `t=5`: `B_r` of weights `7,8,9,10`, `C_r` of weights `13,14,15,16`).
   354	At `t=3` and `t=4` the two generating sets have the same reduced Groebner basis:
   355	`GB(J_t^tail) = GB(T_{t,2t-1}, G_1, .., G_{t-1})` mod 1009, checked on the first
   356	root of `H_t` at each of `t=3` and `t=4` (`tail_ideal_t3.out`, `t4_ideal.out`).
   357	The measured lengths are `90` and `572` on BOTH roots, `= L_3, L_4`.
   358	
   359	At `t=3` the whole GRADED prediction was checked, not only its total: counting
   360	the standard monomials of the Groebner basis by weighted degree gives, on both
   361	roots of `H_3` mod 1009,
   362	
   363	```text
   364	Hilb(S_3/J_3^tail) = [1,1,2,2,4,4,6,6,8,7,8,7,8,6,6,4,4,2,2,1,1]
   365	                   = (1+s^4)[10 choose 2]_s = P_3(s),   sum 90, degree 20
   366	```
   367	
   368	(`graded_series_t3.out`).  The charged reports measure only the tail length; this
   369	is the first check of the full series (4.7) for the tail.
   370	
   371	**Uniform corollaries proved by TAIL-SPLIT alone (all `t>=3`):**
   372	
   373	* the projection `V(J_t^tail) -> Spec P_t` is FINITE (degree 2 generically), so
   374	  `dim S_t/J_t^tail = dim P_t/(J_t^tail cap P_t)`: the hard variable `b3` is
   375	  eliminated uniformly and rigorously;
   376	* `V(J_t^tail) cap {b4 = q_{2,0} = .. = q_{t-1,0} = 0} = {0}`, because the top
   377	  row restricts there to `a_0 b3^2` with `a_0` a unit -- this is the `r=0` case
   378	  and it recovers the charged b3-axis statement (sol56 Sec. 6.1) as a corollary;
   379	* `dim S_t/J_t^tail <= t-1`, with equality impossible unless every `G_r`
   380	  vanishes on a `(t-1)`-dimensional component.
   381	
   382	## 5. Leading forms (card item (1)), and Theorem NO-COPRIME-LEADERS
   383	
   384	### 5.1 What the prompt order gives
   385	
   386	Every `T_{t,k}` is weighted homogeneous, so the *weight* initial form is the
   387	whole row and only Singular's `wp` reverse-lex tie-break selects a monomial.
   388	Since `b4` is the first variable, the greatest monomial of the fixed weighted
   389	degree `4t+1-k` is `b4^{4t+1-k}`, and
   390	
   391	```text
   392	mu_{t,k} := [b4^{4t+1-k}] T_{t,k} != 0   =>   LM_wp(T_{t,k}) = b4^{4t+1-k}.
   393	```
   394	
   395	This is sol56 (6.1).  I recomputed `mu_{t,k}` for every positive band at
   396	`t=2,3,4,5`; all `2t-1` of them are nonzero with nonzero norm, i.e. units
   397	(`tail_audit_t{2,3,4,5}.out` Sec. E), so at those indices
   398	every row's leader is a pure power **of the same variable** `b4`, the leading
   399	monomial ideal of the tail is `(b4^{2t+2})`, of dimension `t-1`, and the
   400	tail is not a coprime-leading-monomial CI in this order.  The card's premise --
   401	that Opus V4 and Fable E2 found the naive leading-form ideal not zero-dimensional
   402	-- is confirmed and explained: the leading monomials are pure powers, but all of
   403	the same variable.  No uniform certificate that `mu_{t,k} != 0` for all `t` is
   404	available (its recursion depth grows with `t`; sol56 Sec. 6), so (6.1) is
   405	conditional uniformly and unconditional only at checked indices.
   406	
   407	### 5.2 The right order does not exist
   408	
   409	**Theorem NO-COPRIME-LEADERS.**  Let `t>=3`, `t not in {3,5}`.  Then there is no
   410	monomial order on `S_t` and no generating set of `J_t^tail` by `t` elements
   411	forming a regular sequence whose leading monomials are pairwise coprime.
   412	
   413	*Proof.*  Suppose `f_0,..,f_{t-1}` is such a regular sequence.  `t` pairwise
   414	coprime monomials of positive degree in `t` variables have pairwise disjoint
   415	supports, so each support is a single variable and each leading monomial is a
   416	pure power `x_{sigma(r)}^{e_r}` with `sigma` injective.  Next, the degrees are
   417	forced.  A regular sequence of length `t` generates an ideal of height `t`; such
   418	an ideal cannot be generated by fewer than `t` elements (Krull), so it has
   419	exactly `t` minimal generators.  The `t` tail rows generate `J_t^tail`, hence
   420	are a minimal generating set, hence the graded minimal-generator degrees are
   421	`2t+2, .., 3t+1`; and any two minimal generating sets of a graded ideal have the
   422	same degree multiset, so `deg f_r = 2t+2+r` after reindexing.  Hence
   423	`wt(x_{sigma(r)})` divides `2t+2+r` for each `r`, with `sigma` injective: a
   424	system of distinct representatives for the bipartite graph
   425	`{degrees 2t+2..3t+1} x {weights 1,..,t-1,t+1}` with edges given by
   426	divisibility.  Now:
   427	
   428	* `2t+2 = 2(t+1)` is the only multiple of `t+1` in `[2t+2,3t+1]` (the next is
   429	  `3t+3 > 3t+1`), so the weight `t+1` -- the variable `b3` -- is forced onto the
   430	  top row `T_{t,2t-1}`, with exponent exactly `2`.  That is consistent: it is
   431	  precisely the row whose `b3^2` coefficient is the unit `alpha_t`.
   432	* The remaining weights `1,..,t-1` must be matched injectively into
   433	  `[2t+3, 3t+1]`.  If `D` in that interval is PRIME then, since
   434	  `D >= 2t+3 > t+1`, its only divisor among the available weights is `1`.  Two
   435	  primes in `[2t+3,3t+1]` therefore both require weight `1`: Hall's condition
   436	  fails and no SDR exists.
   437	* Two primes are present for every `t>=16`.  By Nagura's theorem (for `x>=25`
   438	  there is a prime in `(x, 1.2x]`), applying it at `x=2t+2` and again at
   439	  `x = floor(1.2(2t+2))` produces two distinct primes in `(2t+2, 2.88t+2.88]`,
   440	  and `2.88t+2.88 <= 3t+1` as soon as `t>=16`.  For `3<=t<=15` the bipartite
   441	  matching is decided directly.  QED
   442	
   443	`pure_power_sdr.py` runs the matching for `t=3..400`: an SDR exists **only** at
   444	`t=3` (`8->b3^2, 9->b4^9, 10->q_2^5`) and `t=5`
   445	(`12->b3^2, 13->b4^13, 14->q_2^7, 15->q_3^5, 16->q_4^4`), and no `t` with two or
   446	more primes in `[2t+3,3t+1]` has one.  (The prime criterion is sufficient, not
   447	necessary: `t=9` has one prime and still no SDR.)
   448	
   449	So card item (2)'s alternative -- "exhibiting a regular sequence explicitly (a
   450	triangular change of generators making the leading terms coprime powers)" -- is
   451	impossible for all `t>=6` and for `t=4`, and the pattern is a number-theoretic
   452	accident of the degree window, not a failure of search.  What survives of item
   453	(1) is TAIL-SPLIT: the right change of generators is not one that separates the
   454	variables but one that separates the *b3-degrees*, `(T_{t,2t-1}, G_1..G_{t-1})`
   455	with `G_r` b3-linear, and the right ambient is the rank-2 module `M_t`, not a
   456	monomial degeneration of `S_t`.
   457	
   458	## 6. Theorem DEGREE-BLIND: card item (3) is impossible as posed
   459	
   460	Card item (3) asks for a Bezout-type or resultant bound on the weighted degrees,
   461	uniform in `t`, that forces `dim = 0`.
   462	
   463	**Theorem DEGREE-BLIND.**  The weighted degree data do not determine the
   464	dimension: there are two weighted-homogeneous systems with identical row degrees
   465	and identical variable weights, both occurring as the terminal tail of this
   466	family, one of dimension `0` and one of dimension `1`.  Hence no invariant
   467	computed from the degrees alone -- a Bezout product, a Froberg positive prefix,
   468	the CI Hilbert numerator -- can certify `dim = 0` here.
   469	
   470	*Proof (exact witness, characteristic zero).*  Take `t=2`.  `A_2 = Q x Q` with
   471	factors `y=1/5` and `y=2/5`.  On both factors the tail is two rows of weighted
   472	degrees `6` and `7` in two variables of weights `1` and `3` -- identical degree
   473	data, identical predicted CI numerator, identical predicted length
   474	`L_2 = 2 binom(7,1) = 14`.  Computed exactly over `Q` on each factor separately
   475	(`t2_boundary.out`):
   476	
   477	```text
   478	y = 1/5:  T_{2,2} =  7 b4^4 (16 b3 - 35 b4^3)/640
   479	          T_{2,3} = -7 b4^3 (88 b3 - 295 b4^3)/640
   480	          gcd = b4^3;  a_0 = 0;  lead(J) = (b4^6, b4^4 b3, b4^3 b3^2),
   481	          no pure power of b3;  dim = 1;  V contains the b3-axis; length infinite.
   482	y = 2/5:  a_0 = -28/625, a unit;  gcd = 1;  dim = 0;  vdim = 14 = L_2.
   483	```
   484	
   485	Two systems with the same degrees and opposite dimensions.  QED
   486	
   487	Two remarks.  First, this is the sharp form of the observation already made in
   488	`k16-hilbert-regseq-sol56-20260903.md` Sec. 7 for its own Froberg conjecture;
   489	here it is used as a formal obstruction, and the `y=2/5` tail length `14` is new
   490	(the charged reports record only the *full-cone* length `12` on that fibre).
   491	Second, it identifies exactly what any all-`t` proof must consume: a unit
   492	statement not visible in the degrees.  The weight-0 candidate is unique -- `a_0`
   493	-- and by (4.3) its norm is nonzero precisely off `t=2`.  So the hypothesis of
   494	TAIL-SPLIT fails exactly at the charged obstruction `t=2, y=1/5` and holds at
   495	`t=2, y=2/5` and at every `t>=3`, which is card item (4).
   496	
   497	The failure mode is also explicit rather than abstract: when `a_0 = 0` the top
   498	row loses its `b3^2` term, `b4` divides every tail row (measured: `b4^3`), the
   499	b3-axis enters `V(J)`, and `M_t` is no longer `P_t`-free of rank 2 -- the
   500	rank-2 splitting is exactly what breaks.
   501	
   502	## 7. The residual: eliminant, rank criterion, and its cost
   503	
   504	### 7.1 The b3-free eliminant and its explicit certificate
   505	
   506	For each `r=1..t-1` put
   507	
   508	```text
   509	W_r := a_0 C_r^2 - b_0 B_r C_r + c_0 B_r^2   in P_t,   wt(W_r) = 4t+4+2r.
   510	```
   511	
   512	**Lemma ELIMINANT.**  `W_r = B_r^2 T_{t,2t-1} - G_r (a_0 G_r - 2 a_0 C_r + b_0 B_r)`.
   513	Hence `W_r in J_t^tail cap P_t`, and if `V(W_1,..,W_{t-1}) = {0}` in `Spec P_t`
   514	then `V(J_t^tail) = {0}` (a zero of the tail projects to a common zero of the
   515	`W_r`; over the origin of `P_t` the top row is `a_0 b3^2` with `a_0` a unit, so
   516	`b3=0`).  The identity is verified symbolically in indeterminates
   517	(`eliminant_certificate.out`), hence for all `t,r`, and equals sympy's
   518	`Res_{b3}(T_{t,2t-1}, G_r)` on the nose.  At `t=3`, mod 1009 on both fibres,
   519	`W_r` is nonzero and reduces to zero against a Groebner basis of `J_3^tail`
   520	(`t3_eliminant_modp.out`).

