   577	`t>=5` (the only multiple `3(t-1)` of `t-1` in `[2t+2,3t+1]`), giving
   578	`gamma x^3`.  For `t>=6` the tail therefore collapses on `S` to
   579	`{a_0 b3^2, (const) x b3^2, beta x^2 b3, gamma x^3}`, and dimension `0` there is
   580	exactly `gamma != 0` -- which is the banked `Q-PLANE` / `UNIQUE-POWER` step
   581	`T_{t,t+4}|_S = A2 x^3` (fable5 Sec. 5.2).  So the banked chain steps
   582	`(C_{t-r})`, `r<=5`, `t>=3r+3` are the deepest strata of criterion RANK, and the
   583	same cap `r <= (t-3)/3` applies.  Nothing in TAIL-SPLIT lifts that cap.
   584	
   585	## 8. Computation record
   586	
   587	Single core, sympy 1.12, python 3, no other engine.  Every job carries a
   588	`.resource` file written by `/usr/bin/time` (`wall`, `maxrss_kb`, `exit`); all
   589	seventeen exits are `0` and peak RSS never exceeds 132 MB.  `run_all.sh` reruns everything
   590	except the three `t=4` jobs and the `t=5` audit, which are launched separately
   591	because they are the only ones over a minute.  Wall times below are the recorded
   592	ones.  One job overran the card's 10-minute guidance: `t4_ideal.py` took
   593	608.9 s (8.9 s over), computing sympy Groebner bases in four variables on both
   594	roots of `H_4`; it is reported as measured rather than trimmed.
   595	
    55	`g=g3=et(3d+2t+2)/(6q^3)`, `c=-yg`.
    56	
    57	## 2. The closed indexed form of `T_(t,k)` (task 1)
    58	
    59	### 2.1 The `w`-picture
    60	
    61	Put `w=1/h` and `theta=w d/dw`.  Every polynomial in the charged h-chart is
    62	`h^a f(w)` with `f` a polynomial in `w` whose `w^j` coefficient has weight `j`
    63	in the second grading `wt(h)=1`, `wt(pi)=-t`, `wt(y)=wt(g)=0`, i.e.
    64	`wt(b4)=1`, `wt(q_j,0)=j`, `wt(C_j)=j`, `wt(b3)=t+1`, `wt(b2)=2t+1`,
    65	`wt(b1)=3t+1`, `wt(T(0))=t`.  The charged data are
    66	
    67	```text
    68	U = h^q u(w),        u = 1 + sum_(j=2)^(2t) q_j w^j          (gauge: no w, no w^q)
    69	C = h^(t-1) c(w),    c = 1 + sum_(j=1)^(t-1) C_j w^j,        R = LC, L = h(1-b4 w)
    70	T = h^t tau(w),      tau = g2 + sum_(j=1)^t T_j w^j          (T_t = T(0))
    71	S = h^(2t) sigma(w), sigma = g1 + sum_(j=1)^(2t) S_j w^j
    72	V' = h^(3t) v(w),    v = e + sum_(j=1)^(3t) v_j w^j
    73	utilde = (q-theta)u,
    74	kappa = (1-b4 w)^2 c - y b3 w^(t+1)                        (= Q1/h^(t+1))
    75	psi   = (1-b4 w) sigma - b3 w^(t+1) tau - g b2 w^(2t+1)    (= P1/h^(2t+1))
    76	phi   = (1-b4 w) tau - g b3 w^(t+1)                        (= P2/h^(t+1)).
    77	```
    78	
    79	The five Jacobian identities (2.3) of the charged report become polynomial
    80	identities in `w` whose `w^j` coefficient is the weight-`j` band:
    81	
    82	```text
    83	(D3)  2y (t-theta) tau = g [ (3t+2-3 theta) c - 3 b4 w (t-1-theta) c ]
    84	(D2)  y [ (4t+1-2theta) sigma - 2 b4 w (2t-theta) sigma ]
    85	        = 3g utilde + (1-b4w) c tau - (1-b4w)^2 c (t-theta)tau
    86	          + 2(1-b4w) tau (t-theta)[(1-b4w)c]
    87	          + g b3 w^(t+1) [ c + (5/2)(t-theta)((1-b4w)c) ]
    88	(D1)  2y (1-b4 w) v = y g b1 w^(3t+1) - kappa (2t+1-theta)psi
    89	                       + psi (t+1-theta)kappa + 2 utilde phi
    90	(D0)  Delta(w) := kappa v - utilde psi = y g w^(4t+1)
    91	(gauge) [w^t] v = 0.                                                   (2.1)
    92	```
    93	
    94	Coefficientwise, (D3) and (D2) are the charged Euler recurrences (5.6)-(5.7b):
    95	
    96	```text
    97	T_j = g[(3t+2-3j) C_j - 3 b4 (t-j) C_(j-1)] / (2y(t-j)),      1<=j<=t-1,
    98	S_j = { [w^j] RHS(D2) + 2y b4 (2t-j+1) S_(j-1) } / (y(4t+1-2j)), 1<=j<=2t.   (2.2)
    99	```
   100	
   101	(D1) is solved by `b1` (its `w^(3t+1)` coefficient, pivot `yg`) and then
   102	defines `v` (pivot `2y`); the gauge solves `T_t` (pivot `q/y`); the
   103	`w^1,...,w^(2t+1)` coefficients of `Delta` are the charged pivots
   104	`C_1..C_(t-1), q_t..q_(2t), b2` with diagonals `p_C(t,j)`, `p_Q(t,j)`, `p_b2`
   105	of (5.10)/(5.13)/(5.15); the `w^0` coefficient vanishes by (5.7a).  Then
   106	
   107	```text
   108	T_(t,k) = -[w^(4t+1-k)] sigma_t(Delta) + yg [k=0],   0<=k<=2t-1.          (2.3)
   109	```
   110	
   111	This is the requested closed form: a finite, division-free-until-the-listed-
   112	pivots recurrence indexed by `(t,k)`, with every division by one of
   113	`2y`, `y(t-j)`, `y(4t+1-2j)`, `yg`, `q/y`, `p_C(t,j)`, `p_Q(t,j)`, `p_b2`.
   114	Its sign convention is the charged one (`T_(t,k)=[X^k]E_t`, `E_t=-(D0-yg)`).
   115	
   116	### 2.2 Verification (t=2..7)
   117	
   118	`laurent_spine.py` emits a Singular replay of (2.1)-(2.3) over `A_t`
   119	(`minpoly=H_t` when `H_t` is irreducible; one run per rational fibre when it
   120	splits).  Every step asserts the identities it uses (the `T` and `S`
   121	recurrences, the three leading-coefficient identities (5.7a), divisibility by
   122	`s`, the gauge, each pivot's affinity with a scalar nonzero coefficient, and
   123	the vanishing of every band `>=2t` after the pivots).  Controls:
   124	
   125	- the `t=2` rows on both fibres reproduce the banked `terminal_laurent_t2.json`
   126	  exactly (sign `-E_t`): e.g. the `b3^2` coefficient of band 3 is `-28/625`
   127	  at `y=2/5` and `0` at `y=1/5`, the `b3 b4^3` coefficient is `-77/80` at
   128	  `y=1/5`;
   129	- `pivot_control.py`: for `t=3,...,7` every one of the `2t+1` high pivot
   130	  coefficients equals `p_C(t,j)`, `p_Q(t,j)`, `p_b2` exactly (ratio `1` in
   131	  `A_t`), and the `b1`, `T(0)` pivots equal `yg`, `q/y`; printed
   132	  `PIVOT_CONTROL_t{3..7}=PASS`;
   133	- `ringmap_control.py`: at `t=3` the tests of section 5 repeated with `y` an
   134	  ordinary variable and `H_t` an ideal generator over `Q` give the same
   135	  dimensions, radical power and `b4` power (`vdim` 132 / 18).
   136	
   137	Wall times of the exact replay (number field, `/usr/bin/time`): `t=3`
   138	0.1 s, `t=4` 0.7 s, `t=5` 3.0 s, `t=6` 6.5 s, `t=7` 35 s, `t=8` 3 min 49 s
   139	(max RSS 62 MB); the `t=7` rows occupy 4.6 MB, the `t=8` rows 10.7 MB.  The
   140	modular replay at `t=11` takes 4 min 33 s per fibre.
   141	
   142	### 2.3 Uniform support theorem (PROVED-HERE)
   143	
   144	For `1<=k<=2t-1`, `T_(t,k)` is weighted homogeneous of weight `4t+1-k` in
   145	`(b3,b4,q_2,0..q_(t-1),0)` (charged (6.3)); `T_(t,0)=-c+T^hom_(t,0)` with
   146	`T^hom` of weight `4t+1`.  Consequently
   147	
   148	```text
   149	deg_(b3) T_(t,k) <= floor((4t+1-k)/(t+1)) ;  in particular
   150	  deg_(b3) T_(t,k) <= 2 for k >= t (top tail), <= 3 always;
   151	  b3^2 occurs in T_(t,k) only for k <= 2t-1, with cofactor of weight 2t-1-k;
   152	  b3^3 occurs only for k <= t-2, with cofactor of weight t-2-k;
   153	deg_(b4) T_(t,k) = 4t+1-k  (the pure b4 power is present, section 4).          (2.4)
   154	```
   155	
   156	Measured term counts (exact): `t=3`: 17,16,12,12,9,9 (bands 0..5);
   157	`t=4`: 62,54,47,40,34,29,24,20; `t=5`: 191,171,145,128,105,92,75,64,51,44.
   158	At `b4=0` every row is nonzero for `t=4,5` (the `t=3` vanishing of bands 2,4
   159	is weight parity: all residual weights are even there).
   160	
   161	## 3. The b3-axis lemma (PROVED-HERE, all `t>=2`)
   162	
   163	On the axis `b4=q_2,0=...=q_(t-1),0=0` the grading forces every pivot to
   164	vanish except `q_(t+1),0 = rho_t b3`, and forces
   165	`T=g2 h^t`, `S=g1 h^(2t)+sigma_t b3 h^(t-1)`,
   166	`V=h^e+nu_1 b3 h^(2t)+nu_2 b3^2 h^(t-1)`, `b1=b2=T(0)=0`.  Solving (2.1) on
   167	the axis (`b3_axis_closed_form.py`, exact in `Q(t)[d]/(3d^2-t-1)`):
   168	
   169	```text
   170	rho_t   = -(6dt-3d+9t^2+2t-1)/(6t(3t-1)),
   171	sigma_t = t(2t-d)(3t+1)/(2q^2(3t-1)),
   172	nu_1    = -(3t+1)(3d+4t+1)/(12tq),
   173	nu_2    = t(t-2)(3t+1)(6dt+d-3t-1)/(12(t-1)q^2(3t-1)(3t+2)),
   174	```
   175	
   176	the band-`3t` pivot on the axis equals `p_Q(t,t+1)` exactly (control), and
   177	
   178	```text
   179	T_(t,2t-1)|axis = alpha_t b3^2,   T_(t,t-2)|axis = beta_t b3^3  (t>=3),
   180	T_(t,0)|axis = -c,  all other rows 0;
   181	
   182	alpha_t = -t(3t+1)(27dt^3-30dt^2+dt-2d+6t^3+13t^2-3t+2)
   183	          / (12 q^2 (3t-1)^2 (3t+2)),
   184	beta_t  = t(t-2)(3t+1)(6dt-t-1) / (72 q^3 (3t-1)).                        (3.1)
   185	```
   186	
   187	Norms (`N(a+bd)=a^2-b^2(t+1)/3`, equivalently `Res_y(H_t,.)` up to the
   188	nonzero factor `4q^2` of (1.1)):
   189	
   190	```text
   191	N(num alpha_t) = -(1/3) t^2 (t-2) (3t-1)^2 (3t+1)^2 (3t+2) (27t^3+17t^2+t+2),
   192	N(num beta_t)  = -t^2 (t-2)^2 (t+1) (3t-1) (3t+1)^2 (4t+1).                (3.2)
   193	```
   194	
   195	Positive-integer roots: exactly `t=2` for both (the cubic `27t^3+17t^2+t+2`
   196	is positive for `t>0`).  Hence:
   197	
   198	- for every `t>=3`, `alpha_t` and `beta_t` are units of `A_t` (on split
   199	  indices too), so the b3-axis meets `V(T_(t,1),...,T_(t,2t-1))` only at the
   200	  origin, and `b3 in sqrt( <T_(t,1..2t-1)> + (b4,q_2,0,...,q_(t-1),0) )`;
   201	- at `t=2`, `alpha_2=-14(d+1)/625`, `beta_2=0`: on the fibre `d=-1`
   202	  (`y=1/5`) every positive-band row vanishes on the b3-axis and `T^hom_(2,0)`
   203	  vanishes there too, so the b4=0 chart is still killed by `-c`.  This is the
   204	  banked exceptional behaviour, now derived rather than observed.
   205	
   206	Checks: `ALPHA_CHECK=0`, `BETA_CHECK=0` against the exact rows for
   207	`t=2,3,4,5` (`structure_rows.py`); the specializations are
   208	`alpha_3=-5(115d+68)/17248`, `beta_3=5(9d-2)/16464`,
   209	`alpha_4=-13(625d+291)/205821`, `beta_4=13(24d-5)/72171`.
   210	
   211	Geometrically the axis points are the "z-type" pairs `J(Q,P)=-cz`, and a
   212	nonzero point of `V(T_(t,1..2t-1))` with `T^hom_(t,0)!=0` rescales to a
   213	solution of (8.1); so (8.1) holds iff `T^hom_(t,0)` vanishes on the cone
   214	`V(T_(t,1),...,T_(t,2t-1))`, and the natural sufficient statement is that
   215	this cone is `{0}` (section 5).
   216	
   217	## 4. Why nothing else has a polynomial-in-`t` closed form
   218	
   219	Every coefficient of `T_(t,k)` multiplying a monomial that contains `b4` or
   220	some `q_j,0` is obtained from the pivots `C_1,...,b2`, whose inverses carry
   221	the norms `F_C(t,j)`, `F_Q(t,j)` of (5.11)/(5.14).  The exact b4-axis
   222	coefficient of the first pivot `C_1 = gamma_(t,1) b4` has denominator equal
   223	to the odd part of `F_C(t,1)`:
   224	
   225	| `t` | `F_C(t,1)` | denominator of `gamma_(t,1)` |
   226	|---:|---|---:|
   227	| 3 | `2^4 5^2 59` | 295 |
   228	| 4 | `2^3 3 5^2 113` | 565 |
   229	| 5 | `2^3 3 73 89` | 6497 |
   230	| 6 | `2^4 7 17 163` | 2771 |
   231	| 7 | `2^5 3 5813` | 5813 |
   232	
   233	This is a theorem, not an observation.  **Near-axis uniformity
   234	(PROVED-HERE).**  Fix `W>=0`.  For `t>W` the coefficient in `T_(t,k)` of any
   235	monomial `b3^a m` with `m` a monomial of weight `W` in `(b4,q_2,0,...)` is an
   236	element of `Q(t)[d]` whose denominator divides `prod_(j<=W) F_C(t,j)` times
   237	the listed rational diagonals: by the grading, only the pivots of weight
   238	`<=W` (namely `C_1,...,C_W`) and the axis data of section 3 can enter, and
   239	each is a fixed finite recurrence step in `Q(t)[d]`.  The first instance
   240	(`weight1_closed_form.py`, weight-1 linearization of (2.1)) is
   241	
   242	```text
   243	C_1 |_(b4-axis) = gamma_(t,1) b4,
   244	gamma_(t,1) = (-12dt^2+6dt+72t^3+22t^2-3t+2) / (48t^3+20t^2-t+2),
   245	F_C(t,1) = 4(t+1)(48t^3+20t^2-t+2),                                     (4.1)

