   269	| 4 | 5 | 14 | 7, 8, 10 | 16, 19, 21 |
   270	| 5 | 9 | 34 | 11, 15, 18, 23 | 39, 47, 54, 64 |
   271	| 6 | 13 | 70 | 18, 23, 30, 37, 47 | 84, 101, 119, 141, 164 |
   272	
   273	Precisely, these are \(p_{t-1}(t+1)\), \(p_{t-1}(2t+2)\),
   274	\(p_{t-1}(t+1+r)\), and \(p_{t-1}(2t+2+r)\).  SymPy compared actual monomial
   275	tuples rather than trusting Singular's printed term count.  It also checked
   276	all \(a_r,b_r,c_r\), not only the displayed subset.  This is exact evidence at
   277	\(t=3,4,5,6\), not an interpolation to all \(t\).
   278	
   279	For a form \(F\) of weight \(w\), define its pure-\(b_4\) coefficient
   280	
   281	\[
   282	 \lambda_F(t,r)=[b_4^w]F=F(1,0,\ldots,0).                               \tag{2.4}
   283	\]
   284	
   285	In the charged `wp(1,2,...,t-1)` order with \(b_4\) dominant, full support and
   286	\(\lambda_F\ne0\) imply
   287	
   288	\[
   289	 \operatorname{LT}(F)=\lambda_F(t,r)b_4^w.                              \tag{2.5}
   290	\]
   291	
   292	The complete exact table of \(\lambda\)'s, canonically printed as
   293	`(A*d+B)/D`, is
   294	`box/k16brcr-20260903/support_leaders_compact.tsv`; the longer table
   295	`support_leaders.tsv` also gives every norm (2.1).  It has one line for each
   296	\(a_r,b_r,c_r,B_r,C_r\), including both \(t=2\) factors.  At \(t=3,4,5,6\),
   297	all requested leader norms are nonzero.  For orientation, the first nontrivial
   298	entries are
   299	
   300	```text
   301	lambda_b(3,0) =
   302	 (17243090063480373390*d-21238981495666855551)/58053487971322161500
   303	lambda_B(3,1) =
   304	 (2278007000913733232200497*d-2715311442800994277694247)
   305	 /98014258095453943435556000
   306	lambda_B(3,2) =
   307	 (-1914318319777506349029832717098*d+1539583842758608266161318625969)
   308	 /194999630365446646687194065420000
   309	```
   310	
   311	The size of these scalars is itself diagnostic: unlike \(a_r\) at fixed small
   312	\(r\), they have already accumulated the complete spine denominator.
   313	
   314	## 3. Closed spine recursion for \(a_r,b_r,c_r\)
   315	
   316	### 3.1 Spine substitution and depth filtration
   317	
   318	Write \(u_i=q_{i,0}\), initially retaining \(u_t,\ldots,u_{2t}\) and
   319	\(c_1,\ldots,c_{t-1}\), and put \(L=X-b_4\).  The charged translated arrays
   320	are
   321	
   322	\[
   323	 U_m={q\choose m}b_4^{q-m}
   324	 +\sum_{\substack{2\leq i\leq2t\\q-i\geq m}}
   325	 u_i{q-i\choose m}b_4^{q-i-m},                                         \tag{3.1}
   326	\]
   327	\[
   328	 \mathcal C_m={t-1\choose m}b_4^{t-1-m}
   329	 +\sum_{\substack{1\leq j<t\\t-1-j\geq m}}
   330	 c_j{t-1-j\choose m}b_4^{t-1-j-m}.                                    \tag{3.2}
   331	\]
   332	
   333	With out-of-range coefficients zero, set
   334	
   335	\[
   336	 \mathcal B_0=0,\qquad
   337	 \mathcal B_{m+1}={g(3m+5)\mathcal C_m\over2y(m+1)},                    \tag{3.3}
   338	\]
   339	\[
   340	 S_m={3g(m+1)U_{m+1}
   341	 +\sum_{a+b=m-1}(3+2a-b)\mathcal C_a\mathcal B_b
   342	 +(gb_3/2)(5m+7)\mathcal C_m
   343	 \over y(2m+1)}.                                                        \tag{3.4}
   344	\]
   345	
   346	Then
   347	
   348	\[
   349	 V_m=\mathcal C_{m-2}-yb_3\delta_{m0},\quad
   350	 Y_m=S_{m-1}-b_3\mathcal B_m-gb_2\delta_{m0},\quad
   351	 Z_m=\mathcal B_{m-1}-gb_3\delta_{m0},                                  \tag{3.5}
   352	\]
   353	\[
   354	 N_m=-\sum_{a+b=m}V_a(b+1)Y_{b+1}
   355	 +\sum_{a+b=m}(a+1)V_{a+1}Y_b
   356	 +2\sum_{a+b=m}(a+1)U_{a+1}Z_b,                                       \tag{3.6}
   357	\]
   358	\[
   359	 P_m={N_{m+1}\over2y},\qquad
   360	 R_m=\sum_{a+b=m}V_aP_b-
   361	 \sum_{a+b=m}(a+1)U_{a+1}Y_b-yg\delta_{m0}.                             \tag{3.7}
   362	\]
   363	
   364	Equations (3.1)--(3.7), followed by (1.1), are the closed source before the
   365	high variables are eliminated.  They are the frozen Sol report equations
   366	(2.2)--(2.7), lines 78--159.
   367	
   368	Order the \(2t+1\) high unknowns by weight:
   369	
   370	\[
   371	 z_j=c_j\ (1\leq j<t),\qquad
   372	 z_j=u_j\ (t\leq j\leq2t),\qquad
   373	 z_{2t+1}=b_2.                                                         \tag{3.8}
   374	\]
   375	
   376	Let
   377	
   378	\[
   379	 \Phi_j=[X^{4t+1-j}]R=p_jz_j+\rho_j(b_3,z_1,\ldots,z_{j-1}).             \tag{3.9}
   380	\]
   381	
   382	Weighted triangularity proves that no later variable occurs and that the
   383	coefficient of \(z_j\) is the displayed scalar pivot.  For \(1\leq j<t\),
   384	
   385	\[
   386	 p_j={3te(A_Cd+B_C)\over
   387	 (t+1)(3t+2)^3(4t-2j+1)},                                               \tag{3.10}
   388	\]
   389	\[
   390	\begin{split}
   391	A_C={}&9j^2t+18j^2-54jt^2-81jt-26j\\
   392	    &+72t^3+144t^2+88t+16,\\
   393	B_C={}&-9j^2t-10j^2+24jt^2+33jt+10j\\
   394	    &-12t^3-20t^2-8t.
   395	\end{split}
   396	\]
   397	
   398	For \(t\leq j\leq2t\),
   399	
   400	\[
   401	 p_j={-3te(q-j)(A_Qd+B_Q)\over
   402	 (t+1)q(3t+2)^2(4t-2j+1)},                                             \tag{3.11}
   403	\]
   404	\[
   405	 A_Q=12t^2+16t+4-j(3t+4),\qquad B_Q=2(t+1)(j-t),
   406	\]
   407	
   408	and
   409	
   410	\[
   411	 p_{2t+1}={gd\over2y}.                                                  \tag{3.12}
   412	\]
   413	
   414	The frozen denominator audit proves every \(p_j\) a unit for integer
   415	\(t\geq2\), factorwise at split indices (`k16-terminal-proof-sol56-20260903.md`,
   416	lines 265--327).
   417	
   418	### 3.2 Recurrences
   419	
   420	Set \(x=b_3\).  Every solved \(z_j(x)\) is homogeneous of weight \(j\), while
   421	\(\operatorname{wt}x=t+1\) and
   422	
   423	\[
   424	 j\leq2t+1<2(t+1).
   425	\]
   426	
   427	Therefore it has exactly two possible \(x\)-layers,
   428	
   429	\[
   430	 z_j(x)=z_j^{(0)}+xz_j^{(1)},\qquad z_j^{(1)}=0\quad(j\leq t),            \tag{3.13}
   431	\]
   432	
   433	and no \(x^2\) term is possible.  Substituting (3.13) in (3.9) gives the
   434	requested extension of the spine, coefficient by coefficient:
   435	
   436	\[
   437	 z_j^{(0)}=-p_j^{-1}\rho_j(0,z_{<j}^{(0)}),                              \tag{3.14}
   438	\]
   439	\[
   440	 z_j^{(1)}=-p_j^{-1}[x]\rho_j
   441	 \bigl(x,z_{<j}^{(0)}+xz_{<j}^{(1)}\bigr).                              \tag{3.15}
   442	\]
   443	
   444	This is an exact two-layer recursion, not an empirical fit.  It uses precisely
   445	the same pivots as the original spine and hides no division by a polynomial in
   446	the residual variables.
   447	
   448	For an equivalent closed path form, evaluate at
   449	\((x,z)=(0,\bar z)\), \(\bar z=(z_j^{(0)})\), and put
   450	
   451	\[
   452	 L_{j\ell}=\partial_{z_\ell}\Phi_j,\qquad
   453	 h_j=\partial_x\Phi_j.                                                   \tag{3.16}
   454	\]
   455	
   456	Then \(L\) is lower triangular with diagonal \(p_j\), and
   457	
   458	\[
   459	 v=(z_j^{(1)})=-L^{-1}h.                                                 \tag{3.17}
   460	\]
   461	
   462	There is no implicit matrix inversion: for \(\ell\leq j\),
   463	
   464	\[
   465	 (L^{-1})_{j\ell}={\delta_{j\ell}\over p_j}
   466	 +\sum_{m\geq1}(-1)^m
   467	  \sum_{\ell=i_0<i_1<\cdots<i_m=j}
   468	  {\prod_{a=1}^{m}L_{i_a i_{a-1}}
   469	   \over\prod_{a=0}^{m}p_{i_a}},                                       \tag{3.18}
   470	\]
   471	
   472	with \(m\leq j-\ell\); it is zero for \(\ell>j\).  Formula (3.18) is the
   473	finite Neumann/path expansion of a triangular inverse.
   474	
   475	### 3.3 Closed form / generating function
   476	
   477	Regard the unsolved terminal coefficient
   478	
   479	\[
   480	 f_r(x,z)=-[X^{k_r}]R(x,z).
   481	\]
   482	
   483	All derivatives in the next display are evaluated at
   484	\((x,z)=(0,\bar z)\).  Since \(z(x)=\bar z+xv\) exactly, the chain rule and
   485	(3.17) give
   486	
   487	\[
   488	 c_r=f_r,                                                               \tag{3.19}
   489	\]
   490	\[
   491	 b_r=f_{r,x}+f_{r,z}v
   492	     =f_{r,x}-f_{r,z}L^{-1}h,                                           \tag{3.20}
   493	\]
   494	\[
   495	 a_r={1\over2}\left(
   496	 f_{r,xx}-2f_{r,xz}L^{-1}h
   497	 +(L^{-1}h)^T f_{r,zz}(L^{-1}h)
   498	 \right).                                                               \tag{3.21}
   499	\]
   500	
   501	Equations (3.1)--(3.12) and the finite path sum (3.18)--(3.21) are a literal
   502	closed indexed formula for \(a_r,b_r,c_r\).  Then (1.4) gives \(B_r,C_r\).
   503	This is stronger than merely saying that a program terminates: every entry is
   504	a specified finite coefficient convolution and every inverse is a proved unit.
   505	
   506	There is also a concise generating-function form.  Let \(\Delta_t^\sharp\)
   507	denote \(\sigma_t(\Delta)\) after (3.14)--(3.15), and let
   508	\(\Pi_{[0,t-1]}\) select Laurent powers \(0,\ldots,t-1\).  Then
   509	
   510	\[
   511	 \mathcal Q_t(\xi,x)
   512	 =-\Pi_{[0,t-1]}\left(\xi^{-2t-2}\Delta_t^\sharp(\xi,x)\right)
   513	 =\sum_{r=0}^{t-1}(a_rx^2+b_rx+c_r)\xi^r.                               \tag{3.22}
   514	\]
   515	
   516	Consequently the three coefficient series are simply
   517	\(-\Pi\xi^{-2t-2}[x^2]\Delta_t^\sharp\),
   518	\(-\Pi\xi^{-2t-2}[x]\Delta_t^\sharp\), and
   519	\(-\Pi\xi^{-2t-2}[x^0]\Delta_t^\sharp\).  Specializing
   520	\(b_4=1,q_{2,0}=\cdots=q_{t-1,0}=0\) in (3.22) gives the promised closed

